        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-918",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Unbounded recursive redirect following in `_http_get` (src/picklescan/scanner.py:240-259) gives server-controlled SSRF plus uncontrolled recursion. The function signature is `def _http_get(url) -> bytes:` -- a single parameter, with NO redirect counter, depth limit, or host allow-list anywhere in the body. On `if response.status == 302:` (line 251) it does `return _http_get(response.headers[\"Location\"])` (line 252), where the `Location` header is entirely controlled by the remote server. The recursive call re-enters `urllib.parse.urlparse(url)` (243) and builds a brand-new connection from the NEW host: `http.client.HTTPSConnection(parsed_url.netloc)` (246) then `conn.request(\"GET\", path_and_query)` (248). Two consequences: (1) SSRF -- any host the user scans can 302-redirect picklescan to an arbitrary attacker-chosen `netloc`, including internal/loopback/link-local addresses, and the response body is then fed to `scan_bytes`; note the scheme from `Location` is discarded entirely because `HTTPSConnection` is hardcoded, so the redirect target host is always contacted over HTTPS regardless of what scheme the attacker supplies. (2) Uncontrolled recursion -- a server returning a 302 loop (or a long redirect chain) drives unbounded recursion into RecursionError / stack exhaustion. Attacker-controlled entry: `cli.py:66 scan_url(args.url)` -> `scanner.py:611 _http_get(url)`, and `cli.py:68 scan_huggingface_model(args.huggingface_model)` -> `scanner.py:561/572 _http_get(...)`.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived from source. src/picklescan/scanner.py:240 is literally `def _http_get(url) -> bytes:` -- one parameter, and a repo-wide grep for `302|Location|redirect|allow.?list|netloc|recursion` shows the redirect logic exists ONLY at lines 251-252 with no counter, depth limit, visited-set, or host allow-list anywhere in the package. Line 251-252 does `if response.status == 302: return _http_get(response.headers[\"Location\"])`, where Location is entirely remote-server-controlled; the recursive call re-enters urlparse(url) (243) and builds a brand-new connection to the NEW host via http.client.HTTPSConnection(parsed_url.netloc) (246) + conn.request(\"GET\", path_and_query) (248). Both consequences are real: (1) SSRF -- a scanned host can 302 picklescan to an arbitrary netloc and the body is fed to scan_bytes (611, 573); (2) uncontrolled recursion (CWE-674) -- a 302 self-loop recurses without bound. Reach from attacker-influenced input is confirmed in source (cli.py:66 scan_url -> scanner.py:611 _http_get; cli.py:68 scan_huggingface_model -> 561/572) and in the graph (call at line 252 has reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1; methods _http_get/scan_url/scan_huggingface_model all reachable_from_entry=true). Two refinements to the analyst's impact framing, which do not undercut the defect: (a) conn.close() is in a `finally` whose `try` encloses the recursive call, so every frame's socket stays open for the whole chain -- ~1000 concurrent connections/TLS handshakes before RecursionError, i.e. fd exhaustion as well as stack exhaustion (and RecursionError is caught at cli.py:77, so pure-CLI DoS is graceful; the exhaustion matters for library/service embedding); (b) because HTTPSConnection is hardcoded the redirect target is always contacted over TLS, so plaintext internal targets such as the 169.254.169.254:80 metadata service fail the handshake -- the analyst's \"including internal/loopback/link-local\" is broader than what actually works, though internal HTTPS services remain reachable. The redirect branch is also untested (test_scanner.py:126-130 covers only 200/400; the mock has no 302 case). Advisory exploit check: version pinned to picklescan 1.0.0 (setup.cfg:3), but exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no module/CVE/version comparison was possible; this is a first-party code defect rather than a dependency CVE, so that check is not decisive. Rated MEDIUM rather than higher because the SSRF is blind-ish (only scan findings surface), the initial host is user-chosen, and hardcoded HTTPS narrows the reachable target set."
          },
