        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-693",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "FAIL-OPEN DEFAULT CLASSIFICATION (deny-list architecture): `_build_scan_result_from_raw_globals` in src/picklescan/scanner.py classifies a pickle GLOBAL whose module appears in NEITHER `_safe_globals` NOR `_unsafe_globals` as `SafetyLevel.Suspicious` in the terminal `else` branch, and `issues_count += 1` happens ONLY in the two `Dangerous` branches. The returned `ScanResult(globals, 1, issues_count, 1 if issues_count > 0 else 0, scan_err)` therefore reports infected_files=0, and src/picklescan/cli.py:76 computes the process exit code as `0 if scan_result.issues_count == 0 else 1`. Net effect: any attacker-chosen callable reachable via a module absent from the 57-entry `_unsafe_globals` deny-list is silently graded 'Suspicious', contributes zero to issues_count, and the scanner exits 0 ('clean'). The missing control is an allow-list (default-deny) classification \u2014 the tool's entire verdict rests on deny-list completeness with no fail-closed fallback for unknown modules.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. scanner.py:389-390 assigns SafetyLevel.Suspicious in the terminal else branch (module in neither _safe_globals nor _unsafe_globals) and does NOT increment issues_count; issues_count += 1 occurs only in the two Dangerous branches (382, 386). Line 393 derives infected_files solely from issues_count (1 if issues_count > 0 else 0), and cli.py:76 computes the process exit status as `0 if scan_result.issues_count == 0 else 1` (entry point registered at setup.cfg:31-32, sys.exit at cli.py:83). A repo-wide grep of src/ shows SafetyLevel.Suspicious exists at only two sites \u2014 the enum (scanner.py:27) and the assignment (scanner.py:390) \u2014 so it is never consumed by any counter, verdict, or gate; there is no --strict/fail-closed flag in cli.py. Net effect verified: a pickle whose globals are all from unlisted modules yields \"Infected files: 0 / Dangerous globals: 0\" and exit 0, which README.md:43-46 documents to the operator and to CI as \"scan did not find malware\". The deny-list's incompleteness is asserted by the project's own source: the TODO at scanner.py:190-205 names unflagged code/nested-pickle loaders (pandas.read_pickle, numpy.ctypeslib.load_library, joblib.load, torch.load), and grep confirms none of pandas/joblib/numpy.ctypeslib/importlib/multiprocessing/_functools appear in _unsafe_globals \u2014 each falls through to line 390. tests/test_scanner.py:112 codifies issues_count as the Dangerous-only count, and :346 shows a real case (torch.utils.collect_env.run_and_read_all graded Suspicious because only \"run\" is listed). Reachability confirmed: graph reports _build_scan_result_from_raw_globals at scanner.py:358 with reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0. Scoped honestly: this is a protection-mechanism failure producing false-negative verdicts (CI gate bypass for a malicious model), not RCE in picklescan itself; suspicious globals remain visible with the optional -g flag. Hence MEDIUM, not HIGH. Advisory exploit lookup was unavailable (\"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"); version pinned exactly at 1.0.0 (setup.cfg:3), past the 0.0.23-era blocklist-bypass fixes, so no patched-CVE claim is made either way."
          },
