        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-184",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Detection bypass via incomplete extension allowlist literal: `.npy` is scannable and can carry a malicious pickle, but is excluded from the literal allowlists that gate directory and Hugging Face scanning. scanner.py:207-210 define four disjoint sets: `_numpy_file_extensions = {\".npy\"}`, `_pytorch_file_extensions = {\".bin\",\".pt\",\".pth\",\".ckpt\"}`, `_pickle_file_extensions = {\".pkl\",\".pickle\",\".joblib\",\".dat\",\".data\"}`, `_zip_file_extensions = {\".zip\",\".npz\",\".7z\"}`. `scan_directory_path` (scanner.py:585-590) skips any file whose extension is not in _zip_ \u222a _pickle_ \u222a _pytorch_ \u2014 `_numpy_file_extensions` is NOT consulted, so `.npy` hits `continue` and is never scanned. The identical gap exists in `scan_huggingface_model` (scanner.py:568, same three-set condition). Meanwhile `scan_bytes` (scanner.py:544) DOES route `.npy` to `scan_numpy`, so direct file scans detect it. Note `.npz` is covered (it is in _zip_file_extensions); `.npy` is the specific hole.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. scanner.py:207-210 defines four disjoint extension sets including `_numpy_file_extensions = {\".npy\"}`. The gate in `scan_directory_path` (scanner.py:586-591) and the identical one in `scan_huggingface_model` (scanner.py:568) consult only `_zip_`, `_pickle_` and `_pytorch_` sets, so a `.npy` file hits `continue` (line 591) and is never opened at line 594 \u2014 it is neither scanned nor counted in `scanned_files`. The CPG independently confirms only three `<operator>.notIn` operands exist inside that method (lines 587/588/589) under a single logicalAnd, with no numpy operand. Meanwhile the capability demonstrably exists elsewhere: `scan_bytes:544` routes `.npy` to `scan_numpy`, and `scan_numpy:493-494` dispatches to `scan_pickle_bytes` when `dtype.hasobject`. `.npy` is malicious-capable per the repo's own fixture: init_data_files.py:841-859 builds `data2/dns_exfiltration.npy` from a `__reduce__` payload returning `ssl.get_server_certificate`, and test_scanner.py:181 asserts it is Dangerous \u2014 but only via `scan_file_path`. No test closes the gap: test_scan_directory_path (test_scanner.py:515) walks only tests/data/, which contains zero .npy files. Reachability is genuine, not an arrow-function artifact: cli.py:61-62 sends every directory to scan_directory_path and cli.py:68 sends `-hf` to scan_huggingface_model; the graph reports both methods reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0. Net effect is a silent false negative in a security scanner on its two primary bulk paths \u2014 exit code 0 and \"Infected files: 0\" for a directory or HF repo containing a weaponized .npy, which executes arbitrary code when later np.load'ed with allow_pickle=True. README.md:41 explicitly advertises .npy scanning, confirming this is an omission rather than intended scope. Advisory: version pinned to picklescan 1.0.0 (setup.cfg:3); exploit_search returned \"corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so there is no exploit-reference evidence either way and the verdict rests on source alone. Analyst's line range corrected from 585-590 to 586-591 (585 is the splitext assignment)."
          },
