        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-646",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "COVERAGE GAP inside 7z archives \u2014 `scan_7z_bytes` only selects entries matching `_pickle_file_extensions` (`targets = [f for f in file_names if f.endswith(tuple(_pickle_file_extensions))]`), omitting `_pytorch_file_extensions` ({\".bin\", \".pt\", \".pth\", \".ckpt\"}), `_numpy_file_extensions` ({\".npy\"}) and `_zip_file_extensions`, and it applies no `_pickle_magic_bytes` content fallback at all \u2014 unlike `scan_zip_bytes`, which treats an entry as a pickle when EITHER the extension matches OR the magic bytes match. A malicious pickle stored inside a 7z archive under a name such as `model.bin`, `weights.pth` or any unlisted extension is never extracted into `targets`, never scanned, and the archive is reported clean.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. scan_7z_bytes (src/picklescan/scanner.py:415-436) selects entries to scan with a single filter at line 426 \u2014 `targets = [f for f in file_names if f.endswith(tuple(_pickle_file_extensions))]` \u2014 consulting only `_pickle_file_extensions` ({.pkl,.pickle,.joblib,.dat,.data}, line 209). A grep of all of src/ shows `_pytorch_file_extensions` (line 208: .bin/.pt/.pth/.ckpt), `_numpy_file_extensions` (line 207) and `_zip_file_extensions` (line 210) are never consulted in the 7z path, and `_pickle_magic_bytes` (line 213) is referenced at exactly two places \u2014 its definition and scan_zip_bytes line 453 \u2014 so there is NO content/magic-byte fallback in scan_7z_bytes. By contrast scan_zip_bytes scans an entry when EITHER the extension matches OR the magic bytes match (line 453 for pickle, line 458 for numpy), so the asymmetry is real: a pickle inside a .zip named `payload.bin` is caught by the \\x80\\x04 PROTO magic, while the identical entry inside a .7z is never even added to `targets`, never passed to archive.extract (line 429), and never reaches the scan loop (line 430). The sink is reachable, verified against source not just the graph: cli.py:64 scan_file_path -> scanner.py:605 scan_bytes -> scanner.py:551-552 `_is_7z_file` -> scan_7z_bytes; also scan_huggingface_model admits `.7z` via `_zip_file_extensions` at line 568 -> scan_bytes -> same sink; also scan_pytorch:507-508. Graph confirms scan_7z_bytes.reachable_from_entry = true. The outcome is a silent clean verdict rather than an error: `result = ScanResult([])` (line 422) with `scanned_files: int = 0` (line 41), the loop at 430 iterates an empty list, so issues_count stays 0 and cli.py:76 returns exit code 0 while print_summary reports \"Infected files: 0\". No test covers the gap \u2014 the sole 7z fixture is named `data.pkl` (tests/init_data_files.py:710-713, asserted tests/test_scanner.py:258), a name that happens to satisfy the filter. Corrections to the analyst: the defective line is 426, not 425, and the apt classification is CWE-646 (reliance on file name/extension of externally-supplied file) rather than CWE-184, since this is an incomplete allowlist gating whether content is inspected at all. Version pinned at 1.0.0 (setup.cfg:3); the Metasploit corpus is not indexed in this environment (\"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"), so I assert nothing about a published module either way. This is a detection bypass in a security scanner, whose entire purpose is to flag malicious pickles, hence HIGH."
