          "ruleId": "CWE-409",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "MISSING DECOMPRESSION-BOMB / RESOURCE LIMITS on archive scanning. Neither `scan_zip_bytes` (scanner.py:439) nor `scan_7z_bytes` (scanner.py:415) consults `ZipInfo.file_size`/`compress_size`, enforces a compression-ratio cap, caps the number of entries iterated from `zip.namelist()`/`archive.getnames()`, or bounds total bytes extracted. A repo-wide query shows no `file_size`, `MAX`, or `limit` token anywhere under `src/`. `scan_7z_bytes` additionally extracts to disk via `archive.extract(path=tmpdir, targets=targets)` with no size budget, so a highly-compressed 7z or zip bomb exhausts memory or fills the filesystem of any host that scans an untrusted model \u2014 a denial of service against the security scanner itself.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source: neither archive-scanning path enforces any resource bound. scan_zip_bytes (scanner.py:439-466) iterates all of zip.namelist() with no entry cap and streams each matching member in full via scan_pickle_bytes/scan_numpy without ever consulting ZipInfo.file_size/compress_size (available, since RelaxedZipFile.open calls self.getinfo(name) at relaxed_zipfile.py:54) and with no compression-ratio or total-byte budget; its only guard is `except (zipfile.BadZipFile, RuntimeError)` (line 462), which does not catch MemoryError. scan_7z_bytes (scanner.py:415-436) extracts attacker-chosen members to disk via archive.extract(path=tmpdir, targets=targets) (line 429) with no size budget, filtering targets only by filename suffix (line 426). The analyst understated the amplification: _list_globals (scanner.py:271-274) accumulates EVERY opcode of the fully decompressed stream into a live Python list (`ops.append(op)`, later indexed by `range(len(ops))` at 282), giving roughly 100 bytes of live objects per 1-byte opcode on top of the DEFLATE/LZMA ratio, so a sub-megabyte zip whose `a.pkl` member is a long run of MARK (`(`) opcodes drives resident memory into the tens of GB and OOM-kills the scanner. A repo-wide case-insensitive grep for MAX|limit|file_size|compress_size|budget|timeout|ratio|bomb under src/ yields exactly one hit, an inherited CPython docstring at relaxed_zipfile.py:33 \u2014 so the lead's \"no limits anywhere\" claim holds. Reachability verified in source, not just the graph: cli.py:62/66/68 -> scan_file_path/scan_url/scan_huggingface_model -> scan_bytes -> scan_zip_bytes (550)/scan_7z_bytes (552), plus scan_pytorch (506/508) for .pt/.bin/.ckpt; the graph independently marks the calls at 424-429 and 444-456 reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1, and both functions are EntryPoint{exposure:\"reachable\"}. The threat model is the tool's own advertised use (README:20, \"Scan a malicious model on Hugging Face\"), so the input is attacker-controlled by design. Capped at MEDIUM: availability-only (process OOM / temp-filesystem exhaustion on the scanning host), no confidentiality or integrity impact, and no exploit-reference grounding was possible because exploit_search reported the corpus unavailable for the pinned version picklescan 1.0.0 (setup.cfg:3)."
