          "ruleId": "CWE-184",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "The literal set of global-producing pickle opcodes recognised by `_list_globals` omits the extension-registry opcodes EXT1/EXT2/EXT4. The only opcode literals that add to the returned `globals` set are `(\"GLOBAL\", \"INST\")` (scanner.py:291) and `\"STACK_GLOBAL\"` (scanner.py:293); the remaining opcode literals in the function are memo bookkeeping (`[\"MEMOIZE\",\"PUT\",\"BINPUT\",\"LONG_BINPUT\"]` at 289/296, `[\"GET\",\"BINGET\",\"LONG_BINGET\"]` at 303) and string-type validation (`[\"SHORT_BINUNICODE\",\"UNICODE\",\"BINUNICODE\",\"BINUNICODE8\",\"STRING\",\"BINSTRING\",\"SHORT_BINSTRING\"]` at 325). A full enumeration of every literal collection under src/picklescan/ contains no \"EXT1\"/\"EXT2\"/\"EXT4\" string anywhere. Pickle protocol>=2 EXT1/EXT2/EXT4 resolve a callable through the copyreg extension registry by integer code without emitting any GLOBAL/INST/STACK_GLOBAL opcode, so such a stream yields an empty `globals` set, `_build_scan_result_from_raw_globals` reports issues_count=0, and cli.py:76 returns exit code 0 (clean). Caveat for the verifier: exploitability additionally requires the target code to have a populated copyreg extension registry at load time.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from source. `_list_globals` (src/picklescan/scanner.py:262-355) is the scanner's only opcode-inspection routine, and its if/elif dispatch on `op_name` (287-342) adds to the returned `globals` set for exactly two branches: `(\"GLOBAL\", \"INST\")` at line 291 and `\"STACK_GLOBAL\"` at 293 (add at 342). All other opcode literals are non-detecting -- memo bookkeeping at 289, 296-301, 303 and string-type validation at 325-333. There is no else/default branch, so unrecognised opcodes are silently skipped. A repo-wide case-insensitive grep for EXT1|EXT2|EXT4|add_extension|copyreg yields zero hits in src/picklescan/ or tests/, and a graph query for any CpgCall under src/picklescan/ whose code contains EXT1/EXT2/EXT4 returns row_count 0 -- so the extension-registry opcodes are entirely unhandled, as claimed. Impact chain verified: scan_pickle_bytes (396-411) forwards only that set to _build_scan_result_from_raw_globals (358-393), which increments issues_count solely per set element, so an empty set gives issues_count=0 / infected_files=0, and cli.py:76 (`0 if scan_result.issues_count == 0 else 1`) exits 0 (clean). Reachability confirmed: _list_globals, scan_pickle_bytes and _build_scan_result_from_raw_globals are all reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0; cli.py:main reports reachable_from_entry=false but is the declared console entry point and the call chain is present in source (known arrow/entry under-linking). I DOWNGRADE the severity the analyst implied: per CPython's Unpickler.get_extension, EXT1/2/4 resolve codes via copyreg._inverted_registry/_extension_cache, which is empty by default and raises ValueError(\"unregistered extension code N\") for unregistered codes; the attacker cannot populate it from the same stream without emitting a GLOBAL/STACK_GLOBAL the scanner does catch. So this is a real detector-completeness gap (CWE-184) but is only exploitable against a loading process that independently called copyreg.add_extension -- not true for stock Python, torch or numpy -- hence LOW, not HIGH. Exploit-reference check was unusable (corpus not indexed), so no patched/unpatched claim is made despite the version being pinned at 1.0.0 (setup.cfg:3). No proof-of-concept pickle was constructed or executed; verdict is static-evidence only (no Bash/Write tool was available this session)."
